14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 5–6, 1864","place":"Brock Road–Orange Plank Road intersection, Tapp Field, and the Wilderness of Spotsylvania, Virginia","result":"A.P. Hill’s corps stopped Hancock’s attacks late on May 5 but did not reorganize or build strong works overnight. Hancock attacked at 5 a.m. on May 6 and drove Hill’s divisions west to Tapp Field. Longstreet’s arriving corps counterattacked and pushed the U.S. troops partway back. Burnside did not reach Hill’s flank in time. By 10 a.m., Confederate engineers had found the unfinished railroad that provided a route to Hancock’s exposed left","facts":[{"label":"Heth’s line late May 5","value":"About 6,500 men"},{"label":"Hancock engaged late May 5","value":"About 17,000 men"},{"label":"Hancock’s May 6 attack","value":"5 divisions at 5 a.m."},{"label":"Hill brigades that briefly held","value":"2 of 8"},{"label":"Confederate guns at Tapp Field","value":"16"},{"label":"Texas Brigade entering action","value":"About 800 men"},{"label":"Texans still standing by day’s end","value":"Fewer than 250"},{"label":"IX Corps recent march","value":"40 miles in 36 hours with about 4 hours’ rest"}],"maps":[{"title":"The Brock Road fighting, 3–7 p.m. on May 5","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Hancock feeding divisions west from Brock Road against Heth while Wilcox’s brigades return south and counterattack.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=56bdecb3-562f-4819-8d2e-0a13c9032d61","image":"/images/wilderness-may5-3pm-7pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 3 to 7 p.m. on May 5, 1864"},{"title":"Hancock attacks and Longstreet arrives, 5–9 a.m. on May 6","caption":"Blue arrows mark Hancock’s advance west along Orange Plank Road. Hill’s divisions fall back to Tapp Field, where Longstreet’s brigades arrive and counterattack.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=fc291669-21dd-4391-89af-21a615232472","image":"/images/wilderness-may6-5am-9am-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 5 to 9 a.m. on May 6, 1864"},{"title":"The line stabilizes, 9 a.m.–noon","caption":"The third map shows Longstreet’s divisions stopping Hancock east of Tapp Field, Burnside’s IX Corps stalled in the center, and the railroad route south of Orange Plank Road.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=b0583427-a351-4929-80f1-54ced0bdc400","image":"/images/wilderness-may6-9am-noon-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 9 a.m. to noon on May 6, 1864"},{"title":"The battlefield today","caption":"The modern map centers on the Brock Road and Orange Plank Road intersection. Tapp Field lies west of the intersection, and the unfinished railroad crossed south of Orange Plank Road.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.79%2C38.27%2C-77.66%2C38.36&layer=mapnik&marker=38.2968%2C-77.7442"},{"title":"The Wilderness in the Overland Campaign","caption":"The broader map places the battle west of Fredericksburg at the beginning of Grant’s sustained movement toward Richmond.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map showing the Wilderness in the Overland Campaign"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:22","audioTime":82,"title":"Getty and Hancock attacked near Brock Road","detail":"Getty’s VI Corps division opened the fighting at the intersection while Hancock brought II Corps divisions into position behind it."},{"time":"1:50","audioTime":110,"title":"Mott’s division collapsed","detail":"Two Confederate volleys stopped Gershom Mott’s under-strength division. Expiring enlistments and heavy casualties contributed to the breakdown."},{"time":"2:54","audioTime":174,"title":"Birney’s brigades entered separately","detail":"David Birney’s division supported Getty one brigade at a time and suffered heavy losses, including brigade commander Alexander Hays."},{"time":"4:09","audioTime":249,"title":"Heth faced a much larger force","detail":"Heth had about 6,500 men in line. Hancock already had roughly 17,000 engaged and two additional divisions approaching."},{"time":"4:38","audioTime":278,"title":"Lee recalled Wilcox","detail":"At 5 p.m., Lee ordered Cadmus Wilcox’s division back from its connection with Ewell to reinforce Heth along Orange Plank Road."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"Wilcox counterattacked brigade by brigade","detail":"His arriving formations moved toward the heaviest firing without clear visibility or coordination in the woods."},{"time":"6:26","audioTime":386,"title":"McGowan’s brigade charged","detail":"Five South Carolina regiments took heavy U.S. fire, suffered fire from Heth’s troops behind them, briefly captured a gun, and withdrew."},{"time":"7:36","audioTime":456,"title":"The counterattacks slowed Hancock","detail":"Although they failed tactically, the repeated Confederate attacks made U.S. commanders focus on holding Brock and Orange Plank roads."},{"time":"8:40","audioTime":520,"title":"Grant misidentified Wilcox’s movement","detail":"Signalmen saw a Confederate column moving south. Grant believed it came from Ewell and ordered pressure against Ewell’s supposedly weakened line."},{"time":"9:22","audioTime":562,"title":"Wadsworth moved against Hill’s left","detail":"Grant sent James Wadsworth’s division through a mile of thickets and swampy ground toward the Confederate flank."},{"time":"10:31","audioTime":631,"title":"Wadsworth reached the sector at 7 p.m.","detail":"His leading troops threatened Hill’s left, but a charge by a 150-man Alabama battalion guarding prisoners caused them to withdraw."},{"time":"11:26","audioTime":686,"title":"Hancock stopped attacking for the night","detail":"II Corps began building fieldworks along Brock Road. Hill’s exhausted troops rested instead of fully reorganizing and entrenching."},{"time":"13:00","audioTime":780,"title":"Lee expected Longstreet by midnight","detail":"Lee planned a major counterattack after Longstreet’s fresh corps arrived, while Grant and Meade prepared to attack Hill at dawn."},{"time":"14:28","audioTime":868,"title":"Grant and Meade planned the May 6 attack","detail":"Hancock, Getty, and Wadsworth would attack west on Orange Plank Road while Burnside moved into the gap against Hill’s flank and rear."},{"time":"15:39","audioTime":939,"title":"Burnside’s independent status complicated control","detail":"Because IX Corps reported directly to Grant rather than Meade, Meade doubted it could reach its position on time. Grant delayed the start only to 5 a.m."},{"time":"16:17","audioTime":977,"title":"Ewell attacked at 4:45 a.m.","detail":"The Confederate attack made little progress but disrupted the planned U.S. advance on the Orange Turnpike."},{"time":"16:51","audioTime":1011,"title":"The U.S. Turnpike attack did not develop","detail":"Sedgwick made a limited two-brigade attack, and Warren refused to leave his works until the whole VI Corps advanced."},{"time":"17:44","audioTime":1064,"title":"Hancock attacked at 5 a.m.","detail":"Five divisions advanced west against Heth and Wilcox, whose tired and disorganized brigades could not hold."},{"time":"18:55","audioTime":1135,"title":"Six of eight Confederate brigades withdrew","detail":"Hill’s line collapsed within minutes, sending soldiers back through Tapp Field and past Lee’s headquarters."},{"time":"19:14","audioTime":1154,"title":"Sixteen guns defended Tapp Field","detail":"Confederate artillery fired canister after the retreating infantry cleared its front, but the guns lacked infantry support."},{"time":"20:40","audioTime":1240,"title":"Burnside was not ready to attack","detail":"Hancock learned that IX Corps had not reached Hill’s flank and concluded that timely support could have destroyed Hill’s position."},{"time":"21:12","audioTime":1272,"title":"IX Corps halted after a forced march","detail":"Burnside’s men had marched 40 miles in 36 hours with about four hours of rest and stopped near Germanna Ford while the battle continued."},{"time":"21:55","audioTime":1315,"title":"Longstreet’s corps arrived","detail":"After marching for 35 of the previous 40 hours, Longstreet’s troops came east on Orange Plank Road and entered the fight by brigade."},{"time":"23:18","audioTime":1398,"title":"The Texas Brigade went forward at Tapp Field","detail":"Lee tried to accompany John Gregg’s brigade. The soldiers stopped him and about 800 Texans advanced without him; fewer than 250 remained standing by day’s end."},{"time":"24:40","audioTime":1480,"title":"Longstreet stopped Hancock’s advance","detail":"Fresh Confederate divisions attacked in separate waves and pushed the disordered U.S. troops about halfway back to their morning position."},{"time":"25:16","audioTime":1516,"title":"Hancock requested reinforcements","detail":"At 7:10 a.m., he reported heavy pressure, refilled cartridge boxes, and rebuilt his line."},{"time":"26:10","audioTime":1570,"title":"False reports worried Hancock’s left","detail":"Reports of Confederates near Todd’s Tavern caused Hancock to hold back much of two divisions even though Pickett was near Richmond and Anderson was still approaching."},{"time":"28:23","audioTime":1703,"title":"Burnside stalled near the Tuning farm","detail":"Two IX Corps divisions stopped before Confederate-held high ground while commanders considered how to continue toward Parker’s Store."},{"time":"29:26","audioTime":1766,"title":"Lee gave Anderson’s division to Longstreet","detail":"With the immediate crisis contained, Lee reinforced Longstreet and searched for a way to regain the initiative."},{"time":"29:36","audioTime":1776,"title":"Martin Smith found the unfinished railroad","detail":"The Confederate chief engineer reported an unguarded route through the woods toward Hancock’s left."},{"time":"30:02","audioTime":1802,"title":"Sorrel received the flank-attack assignment","detail":"Longstreet ordered his chief of staff to collect brigades south of the road and prepare them to strike together."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Set the May 6 attack plan and directly controlled Burnside’s IX Corps"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Coordinated Hancock, Warren, and Sedgwick under Grant"},{"commander":"Winfield Scott Hancock","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Birney, Mott, Gibbon, and Barlow; reinforced by Getty and Wadsworth"},{"commander":"Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"IX Corps","subordinates":"Moved toward the Tuning farm but did not reach Hill’s flank in time"},{"commander":"John Sedgwick and Gouverneur K. Warren","command":"VI and V Corps","subordinates":"Faced Ewell along the Orange Turnpike"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed Hill, Ewell, and Longstreet and organized the defense at Tapp Field"},{"commander":"A. P. Hill","command":"Third Corps","subordinates":"Henry Heth and Cadmus Wilcox held Orange Plank Road on May 5"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Attacked along Orange Turnpike before dawn on May 6"},{"commander":"James Longstreet","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"John Gregg’s Texas Brigade and other arriving brigades counterattacked at Tapp Field"},{"commander":"Richard H. Anderson","command":"Hill’s division","subordinates":"Temporarily assigned to Longstreet after reaching the field"},{"commander":"Martin L. Smith and G. Moxley Sorrel","command":"Engineer and First Corps staff","subordinates":"Found and organized the railroad-bed flank route"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Winfield Scott Hancock","role":"Directed the five-division dawn attack and rebuilt his line after Longstreet counterattacked","side":"United States","image":"/images/hancock-division-commanders.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"},{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"Led IX Corps but did not reach A. P. Hill’s flank during Hancock’s breakthrough","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Arrived with First Corps and stopped Hancock’s advance at Tapp Field","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Gregg","role":"Led the Texas Brigade into the counterattack after preventing Lee from joining it","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Martin L. Smith","role":"Located the unfinished railroad route to the U.S. left","side":"Confederacy"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Planned the coordinated May 6 offensive"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Rallied Hill’s retreating troops and committed Longstreet’s arriving brigades","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"losses":[{"name":"Alexander Hays","side":"United States","role":"Brigade commander in Birney’s II Corps division","outcome":"Killed during the May 5 fighting near Brock Road"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/wilderness-may6-5am-9am-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the May 6 dawn fighting","caption":"Hancock’s dawn advance drove Hill’s divisions to Tapp Field before Longstreet’s corps arrived and counterattacked.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=fc291669-21dd-4391-89af-21a615232472"},{"image":"/images/hancock-division-commanders.jpg","alt":"Winfield Scott Hancock with three II Corps division commanders","caption":"Hancock attacked with five divisions and briefly appeared close to breaking the Confederate right.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant planned simultaneous pressure on both main roads but Burnside and the northern corps did not attack as intended.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Robert E. Lee","caption":"Lee used artillery and Longstreet’s arriving troops to stop the U.S. breakthrough at Tapp Field.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 482","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/01/26/civil-war-podcast-episode-482/"},{"label":"National Park Service: History of the Battle of the Wilderness","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/wildspot.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Wilderness troop movement maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery.htm?id=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E"},{"label":"National Park Service: Tapp Field","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/tapp-field.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Longstreet’s Wounding","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/longstreet-s-wounding.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Hancock and division commanders","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670736/"}]}}]}]
